i-militarism.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-11-26T09:32:16Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#45789659177", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Francis Charles Dehault de Pressensé was a French politician and journalist.", "newValue": "Francis Charles Dehault de Pressensé (1853-1914) was a pivotal French intellectual, diplomat, journalist, and politician whose career spanned the tumultuous decades of the Third Republic. Initially a liberal Protestant and foreign affairs expert, he became a leading figure in the defense of Captain Alfred Dreyfus, an experience that dramatically transformed his political outlook, leading him to embrace socialism and anti-militarism. His trajectory from a diplomatic attaché to a socialist deputy in the Chamber of Deputies embodies the profound ideological shifts and moral crises that defined fin-de-siècle France, making him a significant voice in the nascent human rights discourse.

Born in Paris, Pressensé served in the French diplomatic corps from 1879 to 1888, including postings in Washington D.C. and Constantinople, before becoming the influential foreign affairs editor for *Le Temps*. His commitment to justice was profoundly tested during the Dreyfus Affair (1894-1906), where he emerged as one of the most vocal and articulate *dreyfusards*, contributing extensively to *L'Aurore* alongside Émile Zola. This period marked his definitive break with conservative republicanism and his embrace of a socialist platform, culminating in his election as a deputy for the Rhône department in 1902, where he championed secularism, social reforms, and international peace.

